<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macarius, Metropolitan of Moscow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1482–<a href="/years/1563">1563</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint Macarius of Moscow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macarius was Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and All Rus' from 1542 to 1563 and a leading adviser of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>, whom he crowned as tsar in the Dormition Cathedral on 16 January 1547. Earlier archbishop of Novgorod, he compiled the Great Menaion Reader, a vast collection of religious texts, presided over councils in 1547 and 1549 that canonised many Russian saints, and led the Hundred Chapters Council of 1551.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macarius-metropolitan-of-moscow.jpg" alt="Macarius, Metropolitan of Moscow" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian icon painter (before 1917)Public domain image (according to PD-Russia-expired) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macarius_of_Moscow_(19-20th_c.,_Atheism_museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Macarius was born in the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>; his secular name is thought to have been Mikhail. In the late 15th century he became a monk at the St. Paphnutius Monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borovsk">Borovsk</a>, where he served as reader, deacon and priest and learned icon painting, and he supported Joseph Volotsky and his disciples. In 1523 Metropolitan Daniel made him archimandrite of a monastery in Mozhaisk, where he came to know Grand Prince Vasili III, whose divorce from Solomonia Saburova and second marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elena-glinskaya">Elena Glinskaya</a> he supported.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Appointed archbishop of Novgorod in 1526, he followed pro-Muscovite policies and in 1533 and 1535 sent the monk Ilya and others as missionaries to the Finno-Ugric peoples along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a>, Lakes Ladoga and Onega and into the Kola Peninsula. In 1541 he and his companions finished the first edition of the Great Menaion Reader, twelve volumes arranged by month; further editions followed in 1552 and 1554. With the support of Prince Andrey Shuisky he was elected metropolitan on 16 March 1542.</p><p>His independence brought attempts to remove him during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>'s minority, and in 1544 he escaped the fire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Moscow Kremlin</a>. He took part in removing the Glinskys from government, blessed Ivan's marriage to Anastasia Zakharyina-Yuriyeva, and at the zemsky sobors of 1547, 1549 and 1550 urged conciliation between boyar factions. At his synods 39 all-Russian saints were canonised. He blessed the army before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> campaign of 1552, supported heresy charges against Matvei Bashkin, starets Artemiy and Feodosiy Kosoy at the councils of 1553–1555, and conducted diplomatic business when the tsar was away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Late in life Macarius withdrew from affairs of state, supervising the Stepennaya kniga, which traced Ivan's lineage to a fictitious brother of Augustus named Prus, supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-fyodorov">Ivan Fyodorov</a>'s printing and renovating icons. He died on 12 January 1563 and was buried in the Cathedral of the Dormition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Moscow Kremlin</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> canonised him in 1988, though his name appears in lists of saints from the 18th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macarius, Metropolitan of Moscow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 16 January 1547 <b>Metropolitan Macarius</b> crowned the sixteen-year-old Ivan in the Cathedral of the Dormition as tsar, a title derived from Caesar that earlier rulers had used only occasionally.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 16 January 1547 his grandson Ivan IV, then sixteen, was crowned Tsar of All Russia by <b>Metropolitan Macarius</b> in the Dormition Cathedral of the Moscow Kremlin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1534" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Macarius, Metropolitan of Moscow"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5366 5342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5366 5342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1534">What was here in 1534? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
